Abstraction - can be achieved by using abstract class and interface

Abstract Class:
1. A class declared with the abstract keyword is known as an abstract class.
2. It can have abstract methods (methods without a body) as well as concrete methods (methods with a
body).
3. An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly, meaning you cannot create an object of an abstract
class. Instead,it is meant to be subclassed, and its abstract methods must be implemented by the
subclasses.

Interface:
1. An interface is a reference type in Java, similar to a class, that can contain only abstract
methods (methods without a body) and constants (static final variables).
2. Interfaces are implicitly public and all methods are implicitly public abstract.
3. A class can implement multiple interfaces using the implements keyword.

Exceptions in Java:
1. An exception is an event that occurs during the execution of a program that disrupts the normal flow of the code.
2. Java provides a robust exception handling mechanism to handle runtime errors and maintain the normal flow of the application.
3. Exceptions can be categorized into two main types:
   - Checked Exceptions: These are exceptions that are checked at compile-time. The programmer is required to handle these exceptions using try-catch blocks or by declaring them in the method signature using the throws  keyword. Examples include IOException, SQLException, etc.
   - Unchecked Exceptions: These are exceptions that occur at runtime and are not checked at compile-time. They are usually caused by programming errors, such as null pointer exceptions or array index out of

This is the responsibilty of the method to create exception object with help of JVM and throw it to the calling method. The calling method is responsible to handle the exception object.
In Exception object we are having three information:
1. Name of the exception
2. Description of the exception
3. Stack trace of the exception